// HelpAlerts — Alerts (generated from docs/shell/help-alerts.md).
const HelpAlerts = "\x1b[38;5;208mAlerts\x1b[0m\n  An alert evaluates a value from stdin against a condition.\n  When the condition holds, alert writes to stderr — in a\n  watch, that stderr is what drives incidents and notifications.\n  Stdin always passes through to stdout unchanged (tee behavior).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConditions (summary)\x1b[0m\n  - above <N> — value greater than N\n  - below <N> — value less than N\n  - eq <N>, neq <N>\n  - changed — value differs from previous run\n  - absent — no input received in the window\n  - rising N% — increase over baseline\n  - falling N% — decrease over baseline\n  - (no keyword) — \"any\"; fires when stdin is non-empty\n\n  See man alert for grammar, --duration, --average, --msg.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSingle-row vs multi-row\x1b[0m\n  By default alert treats stdin as one scalar per tick. With\n  -k <field>, each non-blank line is one row and <field> is\n  the value to compare. With -t <field>, another field names\n  the row for per-tag incident dedup. <field> is either a\n  1-indexed whitespace column (-k 2) or a .-prefixed JSON\n  path (-k .latency_ms); -k and -t must agree on which form.\n\n    # per-host disk alert (positional)\n    tail /log/shard.log | jq -r '\"\\(.host) \\(.pct)\"' \\\n      | alert above 80 -k 2 -t 1 --notify=ops\n\n    # same alert against JSONL — no jq -r reformatting\n    tail /log/shard.log | jq 'select(.type==\"disk\")' \\\n      | alert above 80 -k .pct -t .host --notify=ops\n\n\x1b[38;5;208m/etc/notify.yaml\x1b[0m\n  --notify=<alias> looks up a destination from your notify.yaml\n  — a YAML map of alias name → per-service targets. Edit with:\n\n    vi /etc/notify.yaml\n\n  Each alias bundles one or more Slack channels and/or PagerDuty\n  integrations. slack: and pagerduty: are always lists; each entry\n  is either a bare string or a mapping.\n\n    ops:\n      slack:\n        - \"#alerts\"\n      pagerduty:\n        - ops-daytime\n\n    critical:\n      slack:\n        - \"#oncall\"\n        - \"#pager\"\n      pagerduty:\n        - name: primary\n          severity: critical\n\n  Slack entries are channel names (strings), or {channel: \"#name\"}\n  mappings. PagerDuty entries are integration names (strings, severity\n  defaults to error), or {name: \"...\", severity: \"...\"} where severity\n  is one of critical, error, warning, info.\n\n  Slack and PagerDuty credentials are configured under Account →\n  Integrations; notify.yaml only names destinations. The PagerDuty\n  integration name must match one you've registered there.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTesting an alias\x1b[0m\n    echo \"test fire\" | alert --notify=ops\n\n  Interactive runs dispatch immediately; they don't open an incident\n  (incidents are a cron-watch concept).\n"

// HelpBuiltins — Builtins (generated from docs/shell/help-builtins.md).
const HelpBuiltins = "\x1b[38;5;208mQuerying logs\x1b[0m\n  - cat /log/<app>.log — stream all lines for an app\n  - head /log/<app>.log — first N lines (oldest)\n  - tail /log/<app>.log — last N lines; tail -f follows\n  - ls /log — list available apps (browse the namespace tree)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mText pipeline\x1b[0m\n  - grep — filter lines by pattern\n  - awk — field-oriented extraction / predicates\n  - sed — stream substitution\n  - jq — JSON filtering and projection (implies JSON source)\n  - cut, paste — column slicing and joining\n  - sort, uniq — ordering and de-duplication\n  - wc — line/word/byte counts\n  - rev, tac — reverse chars / reverse line order\n  - tee, tr — duplicate to file / translate chars\n  - xargs — build commands from stdin\n  - less — page the final output (see man less)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mFilesystem\x1b[0m\n  - ls [-l] [-a] [-h] [path] — list /bin, /cron.d, /etc, /log, /tmp; ls -l /cron.d/ adds a sparkline\n  - cat /bin/<fn> — view a saved function\n  - cat /cron.d/<watch> — view a watch definition\n  - chart <watch> / ... | chart [-x ... -y ...] — render a saved watch or a pipeline as an in-terminal chart (man chart)\n  - > / >> — redirect pipeline output into /tmp\n  - mkdir /tmp/<dir> — organise scratch files into folders\n  - cp <src> <dst> / mv <src> <dst> — duplicate / rename (within /tmp)\n  - touch <path> — create empty / reset the 48h clock on a /tmp file\n  - rm <path> / rm -rf <dir> — delete a file or directory\n  - vi <path> — edit (vim keys)\n  - cd <path> — change the virtual cwd\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWorking on saved results\x1b[0m\n  - diff <a> <b> — line-level differences between two files\n  - comm <a> <b> — sorted-set merge: only-in-a, only-in-b, in-both\n  - join <a> <b> — relational join on a key column (-t / -1 / -2)\n  - paste <a> <b> [...] — column-merge files line-by-line (-d)\n  - wc <files> — -l lines, -w words, -c bytes, -m chars, -L longest line\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mVersion history (wit)\x1b[0m\n  - wit log <path> — list versions; -p includes diffs\n  - wit show <path> <ref> — print a version's content\n  - wit diff <path> <a>..<b> — unified diff between two refs\n  - wit checkout <path> <ref> — make a past version the new HEAD\n  - wit amend [-m MSG] <path> [<ref>] — set a commit message\n\n  Refs: HEAD, HEAD^, HEAD^N, or a hash prefix (≥4 hex chars).\n  Tracked under /bin and /cron.d only; last 5 versions retained.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAlerting\x1b[0m\n  - <pipeline> | alert <condition> — see help alerts or man alert\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mShell\x1b[0m\n  - range — show or set the query window\n  - which <name> — show where a command lives\n  - type <name> — identify a command\n  - history — past commands\n  - clear — clear the screen\n  - exit — quit the shell\n\n\x1b[38;5;8mEach entry above is a command — man <name> where a page exists.\x1b[0m\n"

// HelpCron — Watches (generated from docs/shell/help-cron.md).
const HelpCron = "\x1b[38;5;208mWatches\x1b[0m\n  A watch runs a pipeline on a schedule. Its stdout is recorded\n  as a cron result; writes to stderr are treated as alert firings\n  (if the watch has a | alert stage — see help alerts).\n\n  - vi /cron.d/<name> — create or edit a watch\n  - cat /cron.d/<name> — view a watch definition\n  - ls /cron.d — list existing watches; ls -l /cron.d/ adds a sparkline column\n  - chart <name> — chart recent results (man chart)\n  - rm /cron.d/<name> — delete a watch (stops the schedule)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWatch file format\x1b[0m\n    # schedule: every 1m          (or cron: */5 * * * *)\n    # notify: ops                 (alias from /etc/notify.yaml)\n    cat /log/api.log | grep ERROR | wc -l\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAccessing past results\x1b[0m\n  - weft.latest <watch> — most recent value\n  - weft.avg <watch> <duration> — rolling average over window\n  - weft.max <watch> <duration> — window maximum\n  - weft.min <watch> <duration> — window minimum\n  - weft.sum <watch> <duration> — window sum\n  - weft.stddev <watch> <duration> — window standard deviation\n\n  Example:\n\n    echo $(weft.avg error-rate 1h) | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTier limits\x1b[0m\n  {{TIER_LIMITS}}\n"

